Status: IN_PROGRESS
GitHub issue: https://github.com/Zanark/BowserBoy/issues/101
Milestone: Predictable light switches and a brighter room.
Dependencies: 98 separate Lighting dock; 74 release qualification.
Purpose: Remove unpredictable switching delay without weakening GPU safety
or washing out the DS's approved blue-cyan shell.

Tasks:
- [x] Keep lamp/room shader light counts stable across switches.
- [x] Paint direct-light changes before expensive reflection refresh.
- [x] Bypass artificial recovery delay, retaining actual GPU/native/modal gates.
- [x] Increase room ceiling/fill output while preserving warm colors/exposure.
- [x] Check natural-frame response, rapid last-state-wins input and real pixels.
- [x] Preserve existing DS cyan and strict GPU scheduling assertions.
- [ ] Complete qualification under the bounded CI recovery policy in104.
- [ ] Verify actual GitHub issue/Project synchronization and publication.

Acceptance criteria:
Real lighting responds without queued stale states or extra shader variants.
Actual tabletop pixels are brighter than the preceding120/20 room levels.
DS blue-cyan color and GPU safety retain their original assertions; no reduced
rendering quality, retry or deadline extension substitutes for a passing gate.

Completion evidence:
The natural-frame switching/brightness case and original GPU-fence/native-
gating case pass. A50% increase to180/30 washed out the DS lid and failed the
unchanged cyan assertion. A20% increase was also insufficiently color-safe.
The final132/22 levels increase both original light outputs10%; the actual
tabletop brightness comparison and original warm/room DS color case pass.
No DS shell color/material or renderer exposure was changed to mask that
regression. Full final-source local qualification and publication are pending.

Completion log:
2026-11-07: Owner reports slow unpredictable light switches and requests a
brighter room. Prioritize eligible direct-light paints and defer cube/PMREM
refresh rather than queueing work ahead of the visible response. Retain the
fixed light objects to avoid switch-time shader-count compilation. Bound the
brightness increase using the existing cyan requirement, not a relaxed test.
